[38;5;231m⏺[39m [38;5;153mnode:test[39m only — that keeps the repo at zero dependencies, which is worth preserving.

  One thing still open from section 3: behavior change 1 has a real choice inside it.

  [1mThe fork:[0m if the username leaves the logs and nothing replaces it, you lose the ability to tie a log line to a
  specific user or session while debugging — which is sometimes exactly what "debug production issues" needs. A session
  id gets that correlation back without logging personal data: a random value generated per page load, allowlisted,
  attached to every record from that session. It's about five lines and it's much cheaper to add now than to retrofit
  once records are flowing.

  - [1mNo correlation field[0m — simplest, least data. You can see [3mthat[0m a login failed, never [3mwhose[0m or whether two failures
    were the same person retrying.
  - [1mSession id[0m — correlation without personal data. Costs five lines and one more allowlisted field. Not reversible for
    free later: records already emitted won't have it.
  - [1mKeep logging the username[0m — maximum debuggability, and it's what the code does today. Puts personal data into output
    that any user can turn on from a URL parameter, next to a password field.

  [1mMy recommendation: session id.[0m It buys back the thing you actually lose, and it's the option that stays defensible if
  these logs ever go to a remote collector.

